WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:06.707
NIST Special Publication 800-171 provides security requirements for protecting CUI in nonfederal systems and organizations.

00:00:06.707 --> 00:00:11.993
It is the principal technical and operational foundation for CMMC Level 2.

00:00:11.993 --> 00:00:18.601
The CMMC Level 2 model maps to 110 requirements from NIST SP 800-171 Revision 2.

00:00:18.601 --> 00:00:24.327
These requirements are organized across fourteen families covering areas such as access control,

00:00:24.327 --> 00:00:28.291
awareness, audit, configuration, incident response, maintenance, and risk assessment.

00:00:28.291 --> 00:00:32.696
Other families address identification and authentication, media protection, personnel security,

00:00:32.696 --> 00:00:35.339
physical protection, security assessment, communications protection,

00:00:35.339 --> 00:00:37.542
and system and information integrity.

00:00:37.542 --> 00:00:39.744
The requirements describe security outcomes,

00:00:39.744 --> 00:00:42.828
but implementation depends on the organization's environment.

00:00:42.828 --> 00:00:45.470
Architecture, technology, workforce, contracts, data flows,

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:49.875
and operational constraints all shape how a requirement is satisfied.

00:00:49.875 --> 00:00:54.721
CMMC does not turn the NIST requirements into a product checklist.

00:00:54.721 --> 00:01:01.769
Assessors evaluate whether applicable practices are implemented within scope and supported by objective evidence from policy,

00:01:01.769 --> 00:01:04.412
procedure, configuration, records, interviews, and observation.

00:01:04.412 --> 00:01:10.138
A System Security Plan describes the covered environment and how requirements are implemented.

00:01:10.138 --> 00:01:15.864
Plans of Action and Milestones may have a limited role under program rules,

00:01:15.864 --> 00:01:22.031
but they are not a substitute for understanding current status and completing required remediation.

00:01:22.031 --> 00:01:28.198
Organizations also need to monitor official transition guidance because standards and contract requirements evolve.

00:01:28.198 --> 00:01:33.484
The applicable contract and current CMMC program materials determine which version and

00:01:33.484 --> 00:01:36.127
assessment expectations govern a particular obligation.

00:01:36.127 --> 00:01:38.329
The key relationship is simple:

00:01:38.329 --> 00:01:42.734
NIST SP 800-171 defines the Level 2 security requirement foundation,

00:01:42.734 --> 00:01:46.699
while CMMC adds program structure for assessment, affirmation, status,

00:01:46.699 --> 00:01:48.020
and contractual verification.
